Ladybird gets the Trusted Types compliant string for a script’s
insertHTML value in execCommand() itself, before the command runs,
and never checks the HTML a user pastes. Blink does the same: Its
Document::execCommand() calls TrustedTypesCheck() before EditorCommand::
Execute(), for a command whose IsValueInterpretedAsHtml() is true.
WebKit’s Document::execCommand() calls trustedTypeCompliantString()
for insertHTML before it runs the command. And Gecko’s Document::
ExecCommand() reaches GetTrustedTypesCompliantString() for Command::
InsertHTML only, through Document::ConvertToInternalCommand(). All
three take “(DOMString or TrustedHTML)” for the value, and none runs
a Trusted Types check on the paste path (Blink’s clipboard_commands.cc
and serialization.cc, WebKit’s Editor.cpp and markup.cpp, Gecko’s
HTMLEditorDataTransfer.cpp). While Ladybird gets that string,
an insertHTML call from a default policy’s createHTML callback
returns false. So does Blink, which sets is_running_exec_command_
before TrustedTypesCheck() runs; WebKit and Gecko run their checks
before any recursion guard.
